        <div id="gas-or-battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Gas or Battery: Decide by the Wood You Cut
          </h2>

          <p>
            For homeowners, the gas versus battery decision comes down to how much wood you cut and how big it is. Battery saws have improved enormously; a good 40 to 56-volt saw now handles storm cleanup, limbing and occasional firewood with no fuel, no pull cord and no noise between cuts. Gas saws still win for sustained cutting of large hardwood, all-day firewood sessions, and remote spots where you cannot recharge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery saws are quieter, start instantly, idle silently and need far less maintenance. They are lighter in the compact sizes, which helps control and reduces fatigue. Their limits are runtime and sustained power: big rounds of oak drain a battery quickly, and a full day of cutting needs several batteries and a generator or outlet for charging. Gas saws run as long as you have fuel and bar oil, and a mid-size 50cc saw has more sustained power in big wood than most battery saws.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
