                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best fit</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical saw</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pruning and limbing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Compact battery</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12 to 14-inch battery saw</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Storm cleanup, suburban</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid-size battery</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">16 to 18-inch battery saw</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Firewood, a few cords a year</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid-size gas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50cc gas with 16 to 20-inch bar</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Large hardwood and blowdowns</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Larger gas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">55 to 60cc gas with 20 to 24-inch bar</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Corded electric saws are a budget option for light work near an outlet, but the cord is a hazard around a moving chain and limits reach. Most owners are better served by a battery saw on a platform they already own or plan to grow into, such as the platforms covered in our <a href="/best-weed-eater/">weed eater guide</a> and <a href="/best-cordless-leaf-blower/">cordless leaf blower guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you heat with wood, process several cords a year, or own wooded acreage, buy a gas saw from a dealer and learn to maintain it. If you cut a few times a year after storms and to keep trees off the roof and fence, a battery saw is the better tool for most people: safer to start, easier to store and much more pleasant to use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bar-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Choosing a Bar Length
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bar length is the usable cutting length of the guide bar, and it should match the wood you cut, not the biggest tree on your property. As a rule of thumb, a bar a couple of inches longer than the diameter of your typical log lets you cut through in one pass. A 14-inch bar handles limbs and small trees. A 16 to 18-inch bar covers most firewood and storm cleanup. Bars of 20 inches and up are for large trees and should be paired with a saw powerful enough to pull them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Longer is not better for most owners. A longer bar adds weight out front, makes the saw harder to control, and increases the size of the kickback zone at the nose. A shorter bar keeps the saw nimble and is easier to handle safely, especially for beginners. Most people cutting firewood and storm debris are well served by a 16 or 18-inch bar.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Bar Length by Job</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">12-14 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pruning, limbing, small trees</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">16-18 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Firewood and storm cleanup</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Larger logs, rural property</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24 in+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Big timber, experienced users</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Always use a bar length the manufacturer lists for your saw. An oversized bar on an underpowered saw slows the chain, makes the cut rough, and strains the engine or motor. Each bar also requires a matching chain with the correct pitch, gauge and drive-link count, which are stamped on the bar and listed in the manual.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a tree is more than about twice the length of your bar in diameter, or if it is leaning, hung up, near a building or near power lines, that is not a job for a homeowner saw. Call a certified arborist or tree service.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Engine Size and Battery Power
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas saw power follows engine displacement. Saws of roughly 35 to 45cc are light-duty homeowner saws for limbing and small trees. The 50 to 55cc class is the sweet spot for firewood and storm cleanup, with enough power for an 18 or 20-inch bar. Saws of 60cc and up are for large hardwood and heavy use. Bigger engines add weight and vibration, so buy the smallest class that handles your typical wood.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery saws are rated by voltage and motor type rather than displacement. Brushless motors are more efficient and powerful, and nearly every serious battery saw now uses one. Higher-voltage platforms such as 56 or 80 volts, and high-output batteries on 18 to 20-volt platforms, drive longer bars and cut faster. Chain speed is another useful spec where manufacturers publish it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight matters more than most first-time buyers expect. A saw that feels fine in the store feels heavy after half an hour of limbing, and fatigue makes mistakes more likely. Compare weights with the bar, chain and fuel or battery installed, since bare powerhead weights on the box understate what you will carry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dealer-sold gas saws from Stihl, Husqvarna and Echo come with setup and local service, which is valuable for a first saw. Battery saws from EGO, Milwaukee, DeWalt and Ryobi are sold mostly through large retailers, and service is usually through the manufacturer's warranty process.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety-features" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Built-In Safety Features to Look For
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every modern chainsaw sold for consumer use should have a <strong>chain brake</strong>, activated by the front hand guard and, on most saws, also by inertia during kickback. When it engages, it stops the chain within a fraction of a second. Test it before each use by engaging the front guard with the saw running at idle on firm ground, as the manual directs; if the chain does not stop instantly, do not use the saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other features include a <strong>throttle lockout</strong> that prevents the trigger from being pressed unless the handle is gripped, a <strong>chain catcher</strong> below the bar that stops a broken or derailed chain from whipping back toward your hand, and a <strong>right-hand guard</strong> on the rear handle. Battery saws add an electronic brake and a trigger lockout, and many have a two-step start sequence.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chain type matters too. Most homeowner saws ship with <strong>low-kickback chain</strong>, which has guard links that reduce the chance of the cutters grabbing wood at the nose. Gas saws sold in the US are designed to meet the ANSI B175.1 standard, which includes kickback performance with specified chains, and electric saws are evaluated under UL standards. When you replace a chain, buy the low-kickback type specified for your saw rather than a more aggressive professional chain.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NON-NEGOTIABLE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If the chain brake does not stop the chain instantly, the saw is out of service.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A working chain brake is the most important safety feature on the saw. Test it before each use and have a dealer repair it if it slips or sticks. Never remove or disable guards, catchers or the brake.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="protective-gear" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Protective Gear You Need Before the First Cut
          </h2>

          <p>
            Budget for protective gear as part of the saw. <strong>Chainsaw chaps or protective pants</strong> are the most important single item; they contain long fibers that are pulled into the sprocket and jam the chain if it contacts your leg. Look for chaps that meet ASTM F1897 or a comparable standard, sized to cover from the waist to a couple of inches above the ankle. They are designed to protect against contact with a running chain, but no garment guarantees protection.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wear a <strong>helmet with a face screen and hearing protection</strong>, available as a single forestry helmet system. The helmet protects from falling limbs, the screen from chips, and earmuffs from the noise of a gas saw, which is well above levels that damage hearing over time. Add <strong>safety glasses</strong> under the screen for fine debris. Battery saws are quieter, but hearing protection is still sensible over a long session.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wear <strong>sturdy gloves</strong> for grip and to reduce vibration, and <strong>boots with protective toes</strong> and good tread; chainsaw-resistant boots add protection. Avoid loose clothing, scarves and jewelry that could catch on the saw or branches. Keep a first aid kit with materials for controlling serious bleeding nearby, and never cut alone if you can avoid it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
