          <p>
            A complete homeowner kit of chaps, helmet system, glasses and gloves typically costs roughly $120 to $250 at the time of writing. That is a small fraction of a single emergency room visit, and it is the best money you will spend on the saw.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kickback" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Understanding and Avoiding Kickback
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kickback is the sudden, violent upward and backward motion of the bar toward the operator. It happens most often when the upper quarter of the bar's nose, the kickback zone, touches wood or another object while the chain is moving, or when wood pinches the chain in a cut. It happens in a fraction of a second, faster than anyone can react, which is why prevention and the chain brake matter so much.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prevent it by keeping the nose of the bar away from other logs, branches and the ground while cutting. Watch where the tip is at all times. Start cuts at full throttle with the chain already moving, hold the saw firmly with both hands, thumbs wrapped around the handles, and keep your left arm straight on the front handle so it can resist a kick. Stand to the side of the saw's cutting path, not directly behind it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never cut above shoulder height, never operate a saw with one hand, and never stand on a ladder with a running saw. Avoid cutting with the nose of the bar, including plunge or bore cuts, unless you have specific training. Cut one log at a time, and support logs so the cut opens rather than pinches; when bucking a log supported at both ends, cut partway from the top and finish from the bottom to avoid pinching.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the chain sharp and correctly tensioned. A dull chain forces you to push the saw, which increases the chance of losing control, and a loose chain can derail. Check that the low-kickback chain and bar are in good condition, and replace them when worn.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="felling-and-storms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Trees, Storm Damage and When to Call a Pro
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most homeowner saw injuries happen during cleanup, not felling, but felling is where the worst accidents happen. Do not fell a tree that is leaning toward a structure, has dead or broken limbs overhead, is near power lines, is larger than you have been trained to handle, or is hung up in another tree. Those jobs belong to a certified arborist or professional tree service.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storm damage creates hidden hazards. Trees and limbs bent under tension, called spring poles, can snap with enormous force when cut. Root plates can tip back upright when a fallen trunk is cut free. Broken limbs hanging overhead, often called widowmakers, can fall without warning. If you see bent saplings pinned under a fallen tree, a trunk resting on another, or anything touching or near a power line, stop and call for help.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">POWER LINES</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Assume every downed line is energized.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Stay well clear of any tree or limb touching or near a power line, and report it to the utility. OSHA generally requires workers without line-clearance training to keep at least 10 feet from overhead lines; homeowners should treat that as an absolute minimum.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            When you do fell a small tree in open ground, plan before starting the saw. Check the lean, clear brush, and plan two escape routes at roughly 45 degrees back and away from the intended fall line. Keep everyone else at least two tree lengths away. Use a proper notch and back cut that leaves a hinge to guide the fall, and walk away along your escape route as the tree starts to go.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once the wood is on the ground, a <a href="/best-hookaroon-reviews/">hookaroon</a> keeps your hands away from the saw and your back off the ground while you move rounds, and a <a href="/best-log-splitter/">log splitter</a> handles the splitting without an axe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="chain-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Chain, Bar and Oil Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sharp chain throws large chips; a dull chain makes fine sawdust and needs to be pushed through the wood. Touch up the cutters with a round file of the correct diameter for your chain after every couple of tanks of fuel or charge, or whenever it starts making dust. Hitting dirt or a rock dulls a chain instantly, so keep the bar out of the ground. A file guide holds the correct angle, and depth gauges need occasional lowering as the cutters wear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check chain tension before each use and frequently during the first hour with a new chain, which stretches. With the saw off, the chain should sit snug against the underside of the bar but still pull around by hand with gloves on. A loose chain can derail; an overtight chain wears the bar and sprocket and loads the engine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the bar oil reservoir full; most saws are designed so bar oil runs out around the same time as a tank of fuel. Use bar and chain oil, not motor oil. Flip the bar periodically to even out wear, clean the groove and oil holes, and file off burrs on the bar rails. Replace the drive sprocket when you have worn out two or three chains.
          </p>

          <p>
            If sharpening is not something you want to learn, many dealers and hardware stores sharpen chains inexpensively. Keeping a spare chain on hand lets you swap a dull one in the field and sharpen both later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel-and-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Fuel, Storage and What to Spend
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas saws use a two-stroke fuel and oil mix, typically 50 to 1 for modern saws; use the ratio in your manual and a quality two-stroke oil, since some warranties depend on the brand's own oil. Fuel with ethanol can separate and varnish carburetors when it sits, so use fresh fuel with stabilizer or ethanol-free premixed fuel. Refuel with the engine off and cool, and move at least several feet away from the fueling spot before starting.
          </p>

          <p>
            For storage, run gas saws dry or drain and stabilize the tank, clean the air filter, and store with a bar scabbard. Battery saws need the battery removed and stored indoors at moderate temperatures, partly charged. Always use a scabbard on the bar when carrying or storing a saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compact battery saws run roughly $150 to $250 for a kit at the time of writing. Mid-size battery saws with an 18-inch bar run roughly $300 to $450 for a kit. Homeowner gas saws in the 40 to 50cc class run roughly $250 to $450, and larger 60cc saws run more. Add roughly $120 to $250 for protective gear, plus files, bar oil and a spare chain.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
