        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Too Big a Saw</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A large saw with a long bar feels capable in the store, but it is heavier, harder to control and has a larger kickback zone. Most homeowners cut wood well within the range of a 16 to 18-inch bar. Buy the smallest saw that handles your typical wood; control is a bigger safety factor than raw power.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Chaps and a Helmet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many leg injuries from chainsaws happen in a split second when the saw kicks back or slips during limbing. Chaps that meet a recognized standard can stop a running chain, and a helmet with a face screen protects from falling limbs and chips. Buy the gear with the saw, not after a close call.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting With the Nose of the Bar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The upper quarter of the bar tip is the kickback zone. Letting it contact another log, a branch or the ground while the chain is moving can throw the saw back at your face. Watch the tip, keep it clear, and leave bore cuts to people trained to do them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting Trees Under Tension Without Knowing How</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Storm-bent trees and limbs pinned under other trees store a great deal of energy and can spring back violently when cut. Root plates can flip back upright. If you cannot clearly read the tension, or there are power lines anywhere nearby, call a certified arborist or tree service.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Dull or Loose Chain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dull chain makes you force the saw, which leads to fatigue and loss of control. A loose chain can derail. Sharpen when the saw starts producing fine dust instead of chips, and check tension before every use and often during the first hour with a new chain.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which saw size suits a typical homeowner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For pruning and small limbs, a 12 to 14-inch battery saw is enough. For storm cleanup and occasional firewood, a 16 to 18-inch battery saw or a 40 to 50cc gas saw is the sweet spot. Larger saws with 20-inch bars and up make sense only for big hardwood and experienced users. Buy the smallest saw that handles your typical wood.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are battery chainsaws strong enough for firewood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Good 56 to 80-volt battery saws and high-output pro platform saws can cut firewood, especially softer woods and moderate log diameters. The limit is runtime: a large hardwood pile drains batteries quickly, and you will need several batteries and charging time. For several cords a year of hardwood, a 50 to 60cc gas saw remains the more practical tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What keeps a saw from kicking back at me?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Keep the upper quarter of the bar tip away from logs, branches and the ground. Hold the saw firmly with both hands, thumbs wrapped, with your left arm straight. Start cuts at full speed, stand to the side of the cutting line, never cut above shoulder height, and keep the chain sharp. Use low-kickback chain and test the chain brake before every use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which protective gear is required for running a saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At minimum: chainsaw chaps or protective pants meeting a recognized standard such as ASTM F1897, a helmet with a face screen, hearing protection, safety glasses, sturdy gloves and boots with protective toes. Keep a first aid kit that can control serious bleeding, avoid loose clothing, and do not cut alone when you can help it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When does a saw chain need filing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sharpen whenever the saw starts producing fine dust instead of chips or needs pushing to cut. For many homeowners that means a few file strokes per cutter after every couple of tanks of fuel or battery charges. Any contact with dirt or rocks dulls the chain immediately. Keeping a spare chain lets you swap and sharpen later.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I cut down a tree myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Small trees in open ground can be felled by a homeowner with proper training, gear, a planned notch and back cut, and clear escape routes. Leave any tree that leans toward a building, has dead limbs overhead, is near power lines, is hung in another tree or is larger than your experience to a certified arborist. Mistakes in felling are often severe.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What fuel mix does a chainsaw use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most modern two-stroke chainsaws use a 50 to 1 gasoline and two-stroke oil mix, but always follow the ratio in your owner's manual. Use a quality oil designed for air-cooled two-stroke engines, fresh fuel with stabilizer or ethanol-free premixed fuel, and never use straight gas, which can destroy the engine in minutes.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

